# Which tier each file in packages/zts/src belongs to.
#
# Four tiers, lowest first. A file may import its own tier and any tier below
# it, never one above:
#
#   zts-base       utilities every tier names and that name nothing themselves
#   zts-contracts  contract and receipt data with its serialization
#   zts            the engine: values, GC, objects, bytecode, interpreter,
#                  parser, builtins, virtual modules
#   zts-compiler   everything that decides whether a program is proven
#
# Enforced in both directions by scripts/check-zts-layering.sh, which
# `zig build test` depends on: an upward import fails, and so does a file with
# no row or a row with no file. The second half is the input floor - a manifest
# that lost its rows would otherwise report zero violations while checking
# nothing.
#
# This is the assignment the four-module split will use. It is checked while the
# package is still one module, so the split is known to compile before
# packages/zts/build.zig is touched. Moving a file between tiers is a deliberate
# edit: say why in the commit, and expect the gate to name whatever the move
# breaks. See docs/plans/2026-08-07-021-zts-three-module-split-plan.md.
#
# Same-tier imports are unconstrained. This file pins the direction between
# tiers, not the size of any tier's surface; scripts/module-boundary.allow does
# that, for consumer packages.

# zts-base
zts-base compat.zig
zts-base json_utils.zig
zts-base json_wire.zig
zts-base known_globals.zig
zts-base module_authorization.zig
zts-base module_slots.zig
